## Bandit Level 6 → Level 7


**Challenge:** 

The password for the next level is stored somewhere on the server and has all of the following properties:

owned by user bandit7
owned by group bandit6
33 bytes in size

**Solution & Explanation:****
```
STEP 1: ls
# quick check of what’s in your current home directory
# (the password is NOT in your home folder for this level)

STEP 2: find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 2>/dev/null
# find → search for something
# / → start searching from the root of the whole system (everywhere)
# -type f → only look for files (not folders)
# -user bandit7 → file owner must be bandit7
# -group bandit6 → file group must be bandit6
# -size 33c → exactly 33 bytes (c = bytes)
# 2>/dev/null → hides permission denied errors so output stays clean

STEP 3: cat /var/lib/dpkg/info/bandit7.password
# open the file that matches the requirements
# it contains the password for bandit7


```
**Password:** [morbNTDkSW6jIlUc0ymOdMaLnOlFVAaj]


**SINGLE COMMAND LINE to find desired file within the folder of interest**

find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 2>/dev/null -exec cat {} \;


**What I learned:** 

2>/dev/null

👉 “Send error messages away so they don’t clutter my results”

Very important part:

2> → redirects stderr (error output)

/dev/null → a “black hole” file that discards whatever you send into it

So when find hits folders you can’t access, you don’t get spammed with Permission denied messages.
